From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 16:56:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: give every network its own stable MAC address

ConnMan associates with every WiFi network using the hardware MAC
address and lets wpa_supplicant scan with it too, so any network, and
anyone listening to probe requests, can follow a device from place to
place and link its visits to different networks.

Add a [General] WiFiMACRandomization option, off by default. When it is
on:

 * Each network gets an address of its own: HMAC-SHA256 over the SSID
   and the security type, keyed with 32 random bytes made on first use
   in STORAGEDIR/wifi-mac.secret, truncated to six bytes and made
   locally administered unicast. The same network sees the same address
   on every connection and after every reboot, so captive portals and
   DHCP reservations keep working, while two networks cannot tell they
   saw the same device. wpa_supplicant applies it through its per-network
   mac_addr=3 / mac_value policy.

 * Scans and probe requests come from random addresses, through
   wpa_supplicant's MACAddressRandomizationMask property, for each of
   scan, sched_scan and pno that the driver supports.

 * A network can opt out with the new UseDeviceMACAddress service
   property, stored with the service and reset by Remove(); it is then
   joined with the hardware address (mac_addr=0). The service hands it
   to the wifi plugin as the network flag WiFi.UseDeviceMAC, which
   src/network.c stores next to the other WiFi flags.

The secret file is opened relative to the storage directory with
O_NOFOLLOW and only trusted as a regular, private, singly linked file of
the right size owned by connmand. Anything else is replaced by a new
secret written to a temporary file and renamed over it, so a symlink is
never followed for reading or writing.

wpa_supplicant gives up on an association silently when the driver
refuses the address, which would leave the connection hanging. Many
vendor drivers do refuse. So before wpa_supplicant takes the interface,
connmand tries the same down/SIOCSIFHWADDR/up sequence once with a
throw-away address and puts the hardware address back. If it fails, it
logs once and joins every network with the hardware address; nothing is
sent to wpa_supplicant in that case, nor when its interface uses a
driver other than nl80211, the only one that can set the address. The
answer and the hardware address are kept in STATEDIR for the rest of the
boot. That record also lets a restarted connmand put the hardware address
back before rtnl creates the device: the device ident, and with it every
saved WiFi service, is named after the address the interface has when
connmand starts.

The record, STATEDIR/wifi-hwaddr-<ifname>, is also how other programs
can show the hardware address while the interface carries a network's
own one, so its format is fixed and documented in src/wifi-mac.h: a
root-owned regular file, at most 0644, of exactly 20 bytes,
"xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx F\n" with lower-case hex and F the 0/1 changeable
flag, replaced atomically.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
